<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Adelita</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;La Adelita&quot; is one of the most famous corridos, or narrative ballads, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, and it has been adapted many times. It was inspired by Adela Velarde Pérez, a woman who joined the Maderista movement in the early stages of the revolution.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Adela Velarde Pérez was a Chihuahuense woman who became a popular icon and a symbol of the part women played in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. In time the figure of the adelita became a byword for the soldadera, the woman in a military-support and sometimes fighting role who provisioned the armies, spied and took part in other ways in the struggle against the federal government's forces. The song presents Adelita as pretty, desired by many of the soldiers, brave and commanding respect.</p><p>The women behind the figure ranged from young girls to the middle-aged and came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a>, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> and elsewhere, often, by some observers' accounts, of mestiza or indigenous origin. Some joined out of poverty, some were forced to the front as cooks when President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a> raised the quota for his army, and many went with husbands who were drafted or enlisted. Besides cooking, they served as spies, nurses, messengers and smugglers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> arms across the border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song, the portrait and the role of its subject have been read in different and often conflicting ways. One argument holds that La Adelita expressed the sensitivity and vulnerability of men, stressing the stoicism of the rebel soldier facing death. The feminist scholar María Herrara-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a> argues that Adelita's bravery and revolutionary spirit are lost in the fatalism and insecurities of male soldiers focused on passion, love and desire as they face combat.</p><p>In corridos Adelita was often reduced to the stereotype of the caretaker or the lover, and male writers and artists reshaped her to fit the era's ideal of a woman both obedient and sexualised, as in Angel Martin's picture of her in a revealing top with a belt of bullets. The song helped make soldaderas seem only romantic figures. Dance has kept a braver image: the folkloric dance las Adelitas, performed only by women, is popular at Chicano festivals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Adelita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
